---
import '../styles/global.css';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Navigation from '../components/Navigation.astro';
import BackButton from '../components/BackButton.astro';
import MindfulnessMeditation from '../components/MindfulnessMeditation.tsx';
---

<BaseLayout 
  title="Mindfulness & Body Scan Meditation for Anxiety Relief" 
  description="Release tension and calm overwhelmed thoughts with guided body scan meditation, mindful anxiety de-escalation, and serene focus timers."
>
  <div class="page">
    <div class="page__header">
      <BackButton />
      <h1 class="page__title">Mindfulness & Meditation</h1>
      <p class="page__desc">Release physical tension, settle racing thoughts</p>
    </div>
    <MindfulnessMeditation client:load />
  </div>

  <article class="article-guide">
    <div class="article-guide__inner">
      <h2>Mindfulness and Body Scan Meditation for Deep Sleep</h2>
      <p>
        Many people assume sleep is purely a mental event. However, sleep researchers have long known that chronic sleep onset latency is deeply somatic: emotional tension and cognitive anxiety are physically stored in muscle fibers. When your jaw is clenched, your shoulders are hunched, or your abdomen is guarded, your peripheral nervous system continually feeds alert signals back into the amygdala.
      </p>
      <p>
        Mindfulness and systematic body scanning reverse this feedback loop. Originally pioneered by Dr. Edmund Jacobson in the 1930s through Progressive Muscle Relaxation and adapted into modern Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction (MBSR) by Dr. Jon Kabat-Zinn, directing non-judgmental awareness to physical sensations discharges residual neuromuscular tension.
      </p>

      <div class="guide-callout">
        <strong>The Somatic Feedback Loop:</strong> A tense body keeps the brain awake, while a physically released body tells the brain that safety has been achieved and rest may commence.
      </div>

      <h3>Three Dimensions of Bedtime Mindfulness</h3>
      <div class="guide-card-grid">
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h4>1. Systematic Body Scanning</h4>
          <p>Moving conscious awareness sequentially through your body, releasing microscopic clenches in the brow, jaw, neck, chest, belly, and toes.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h4>2. Mindful Anxiety De-escalation</h4>
          <p>Practicing cognitive defusion by observing anxious thoughts as temporary passing clouds rather than urgent truths that require solving.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h4>3. Serene Focus Timing</h4>
          <p>Providing a gentle auditory anchor through soothing ambient soundscapes, preventing your brain from seeking stimulation elsewhere in the quiet bedroom.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Step-by-Step Bedtime Body Scan Ritual</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Settle into your mattress:</strong> Lie flat on your back with legs uncrossed and arms resting gently by your sides. Feel the downward pull of gravity supporting your entire weight.</li>
        <li><strong>Release the facial mask:</strong> Smooth the skin across your forehead. Release the tension between your eyebrows, unlock your jaw, and let your tongue rest loosely on the floor of your mouth.</li>
        <li><strong>Scan downward:</strong> Gently guide your attention through your shoulders, down each arm to the fingertips, through the chest and belly, and down through your legs to the soles of your feet. With every slow exhale, imagine that zone sinking an inch deeper into the bed.</li>
        <li><strong>Adopt non-judgmental observation:</strong> When racing thoughts inevitably arise, do not fight them or criticize yourself. Simply acknowledge: "I am having a thought," and guide your awareness back to the physical sensation of breathing.</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="guide-disclaimer">
        <strong>Wellness Notice:</strong> Mindfulness practices are complementary relaxation tools. They are not intended as clinical psychiatric psychotherapy or treatment for acute trauma, panic disorder, or chronic insomnia. Consult a licensed therapist or physician for specialized clinical support.
      </div>

      <h3>Frequently Asked Questions</h3>
      <div class="faq-list">
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How does a body scan help you fall asleep?</summary>
          <p>A body scan systematically brings awareness to hidden areas of physical tension. Consciously relaxing these muscle groups decreases sympathetic nerve activity, slows the pulse, and shifts brainwaves from active beta to relaxed alpha frequencies.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What if I cannot stop thinking while trying to meditate?</summary>
          <p>The objective of bedtime meditation is not to force a blank mind, but to change your relationship with your thoughts. Instead of engaging with every worry, practice letting them pass like ripples on water while anchoring your attention on breathing sensations.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Should I meditate sitting up or lying down before sleep?</summary>
          <p>For daytime meditation, sitting upright helps maintain alertness. However, for bedtime sleep meditation, lying down in your natural resting posture is ideal because drifting directly into sleep is the desired outcome.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How long should a sleep meditation session last?</summary>
          <p>A focused session lasting between 10 and 20 minutes is optimal. This provides enough duration to systematically relax the major muscle groups without creating fatigue or pressure to perform.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Is it okay if I fall asleep in the middle of the meditation?</summary>
          <p>Yes, absolutely! Falling asleep mid-meditation is the ultimate goal of bedtime mindfulness. It is a sign that your nervous system felt completely safe and surrendered to sleep naturally.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
  <Navigation slot="nav" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page {
    max-width: 32rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 1rem 0.5rem 6rem;
  }

  .page__header {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    padding: 0 0.5rem;
  }

  .page__title {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 0.75rem 0 0.25rem;
    color: var(--color-drift-text);
  }

  .page__desc {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-drift-text-muted);
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
